WEBVTT

afferent-light
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Light entering the eye starts a signal in the afferent, or sensory, pathway.

afferent-brain
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Follow the coloured pathways (retina, optic nerve, chiasm, tract and nuclei).

efferent-definition
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:39.000
The efferent pathway is the opposite of the afferent pathway: it carries signals from the brain to the eye.

parasympathetic
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:49.000
The parasympathetic pathway passes through the third cranial nerve and the ciliary ganglion.

constrictor
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.000
It supplies the pupil constrictor and the ciliary muscle.

third-nerve
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:03.000
The third cranial nerve also supplies eye movements and the main eyelid muscle.

sympathetic
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:13.000
The sympathetic pathway passes through the sympathetic chain and the superior cervical ganglion.

dilator
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:23.000
It supplies the pupil dilator and a minor eyelid muscle, as shown in the diagram.

reflex-input
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Light in one eye sends an afferent signal towards the brain.

reflex-output
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Efferent signals then travel to both pupils, causing them to constrict in response to light.

reflex-direct
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.500
The illuminated pupil has a direct response.

reflex-consensual
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.000
The other pupil has a consensual response.

observe
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Observe the size, shape and symmetry of both pupils.

compare-observation
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Compare normal pupils with changes in colour or shape, unequal pupil size and eyelids.

light
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Switch on the Arclight and select the brightest light setting. Use a quiet, dim room.

distance
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Ask the patient to look at a distant target.

clear-view
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:32.000
Make sure they do not look at the examiner or the light itself.

move-light
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Move the light from the side towards the pupil.

watch
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Watch the illuminated pupil for the direct light response.

shine-constrict
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Shine the light into the eye and watch the pupil constrict.

compare-responses
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Look for reduced or absent constriction.

corresponding-examples
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:04.000
The pupil responses on the left may be associated with the conditions at the back of the eye shown on the right.

pupil-colour
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Pupil colour does not determine the light response.

cataract-constriction
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:28.000
A cataract may make the pupil look grey or white, but the pupil should still constrict if the light reflex pathway is intact.

consensual-first
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Shine light into one eye and watch the other pupil constrict. This is the consensual response.

consensual-other
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Repeat from the other side, watching the pupil that is not being directly illuminated.

swing-method
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Move the light from one eye to the other, comparing the pupil responses.

swing-return
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:10.000
Swing the light back and observe both pupils each time the illuminated eye changes.

swing-normal
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:20.000
In the normal example, the pupil remains constricted when the light returns to this eye.

swing-rapd
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:34.000
A relative afferent pupillary defect, or RAPD, means a weaker light signal from one eye. Here, the pupils paradoxically dilate when light moves to the affected right eye.

near-distance
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:43.000
First ask the patient to look at a distant target.

near-target
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Then ask them to look at a near target, as shown here with the Arclight.

near-normal
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:05.000
When looking at a near target, both eyes normally turn inward together. This is called convergence.

near-abnormal
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:15.000
If the eyes do not turn inward together for a near target, further examination is needed.
